# ── THE FOUR COMMENT TAGS, DECLARED FOR THE FIRST TIME, 2026-09-23 ──────────────────────────────
# Until 2026-09-23 this gate matched `TODO` / `FIXME` / `XXX` / `HACK` WITH NOTHING. The playbook
# (docs/design/playbook/gate-no-deferral.md §1b) has listed all four under Class B since the gate was
# specified; xtask/src/gates/no_deferral.rs carried the five PHRASE forms and none of the tags. So the
# four rows below are not new markers — they are markers that were always there, in the marker class
# the rule is NAMED after, in front of an instrument that could not see them. `1.6.0-LEDGER.md` G55
# called it "the headline blind spot" and it was right.
#
# THEY ARE NOT `hot/` ROWS, AND THAT IS THE POINT. `no-deferral-strict-done` FAILS while any non-hot
# row stands, so the release done-oracle is now RED with four named reasons instead of green with
# none. That is the correct reading of BUSBAR-1.6.0.md:1218, which already lists NO-DEFERRAL among
# the RED drift groups — before today the gate and the spec disagreed and the gate was wrong.
#
# WHY WAIVED AND NOT FIXED. Two of the four (N3) are stale or mislabelled prose whose repair is a
# one-word relabel. §3 of the playbook is explicit that this gate "cannot be satisfied by renaming a
# marker", and an agent that renames `TODO:` to `NOTE:` to turn its own finding green has performed
# precisely the evasion the gate exists to resist — however sound its reasoning. Declaring a marker
# with a reason, an owner and a retirement condition is what this file is FOR; erasing it is not.
#
# RETIREMENT, AS COMMANDS THAT RUN AGAINST THIS TREE (no row names a document):
#   N1  grep -n 'Signal::CandidateLatencyP95Ms' crates/busbar-llm/src/engine/hooks.rs  # non-empty => computed
#   N2  grep -n 'Signal::ResponseTokensOut'     crates/busbar-llm/src/engine/hooks.rs  # non-empty => computed
#   (for N1/N2 the other honest retirement is deletion from `Signal::ALL`, which drops the whole row)
#   N3  the owner rules; the marker then either goes away or stops being spelled as a deferral tag
